# Vercel Web Analytics Configuration

## Summary
Updated Vercel Web Analytics configuration to use the correct Next.js-specific import path as per the latest official documentation.

## Changes Made

### Modified Files:
- **app/layout.tsx** - Updated Analytics import path

### Specific Changes:
1. Changed Analytics import from `@vercel/analytics/react` to `@vercel/analytics/next`
   - The project was already using `@vercel/analytics` (version 2.0.1)
   - The Analytics component was already properly placed in the root layout
   - Only the import path needed correction to use the Next.js-optimized version

## Implementation Details

According to the official Vercel documentation (https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart), Next.js projects using the App Router should import Analytics from `@vercel/analytics/next` rather than `@vercel/analytics/react`. The Next.js-specific import is optimized for Next.js environments and includes Next.js-specific integrations.

The Analytics component remains correctly positioned in the root layout file (app/layout.tsx) at the bottom of the body tag, which ensures it tracks all page views across the application.

## Verification

All verification steps completed successfully:
- ✅ TypeScript type checking passed (npm run typecheck)
- ✅ Production build completed successfully (npm run build)
- ✅ All 17 tests passed (npm test)
- ✅ No new dependencies required (@vercel/analytics@2.0.1 already installed)
- ✅ No build artifacts or unintended files modified

## Next Steps

Once deployed to Vercel:
1. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard's Analytics section
2. Verify analytics are being collected by checking the browser's Network tab for requests to `/<unique-path>/view`
3. Monitor the analytics dashboard for page views and visitor data

## Notes
- The package @vercel/analytics was already installed in the project
- No additional configuration or environment variables are required
- Analytics will automatically start tracking once the app is deployed to Vercel
